{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792523793000-architect-host-boundary-review-r7.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792523949000-architect-host-boundary-r8.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792523065000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; git -C .claude/worktrees/host-h0 diff --stat'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792523793000-architect-host-boundary-review-r7.gpt-6-astra.findings.md collab/1792523949000-architect-host-boundary-r8.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md collab/1792523065000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md; cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md; git -C .claude/worktrees/host-h0 diff --stat'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-21 +07  \nCoding-Agent: codex  \nRole: Architect, read-only review  \nScope: r7, §2 and H0-H4  \nReconciliation verdict: **CONFIRMED WITH DEFECTS**  \nArchitecture verdict: **SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES**\n\nThe 9 profile-consumer / 6 retained-arm / 1 deferred-entropy classification is sound. I opened all 16 sites. The two new behaviour changes belong in H2 as explicit portability corrections, with consumer tests and compatibility decisions recorded. They are substantive architectural reconciliation, beyond r4’s sign-off and cmd’s mechanical confirmation. Removing the public encoding arity requires an owner decision; absence of repository callers does not establish compatibility.\n\n`H` below means [dao.stream.host-boundary.md](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md).\n\n| ID | Severity | File:line | Evidence | Correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R7-1 | High | H:437,570,603 | PHP’s floating parser (`yang/php.cljc:95-97`) has no replacement in the exact profile. Table B also lacks the promised portable `code-points`. | Specify float parsing’s implementation, law and oracle; add `code-points`’ destination and tests. Reconcile 76 entries if expanding the profile. |\n| R7-2 | Medium | H:443,742,783,1253 | The two new changes lack explicit H2 consumer acceptance tests; §§6/14 still say “two behaviour changes,” referring to the earlier pair. JVM UTF-8 decoding also substitutes malformed input today. | Name all four changes and test non-ASCII/malformed bytes, `9007199254740993`, overflow and carrier handling. Preserve encoding arity pending owner approval. |\n| R7-3 | Medium | H:440; `src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:78` | Index deliberately excludes Dart doubles from native comparison because signed-zero ordering differs. A uniform `host-float?` replacement would lose this protection. | Specify canonical numeric comparison or preserve the exclusion; require signed-zero tests. |\n| R7-4 | Medium | H:898 | “No tool change” conflicts with generated closure classes: tags originate in `closure.clj:112`; closure EDNs never used `:h0-unlisted`. Current worktree already updates classification data. | Permit classification-data edits and regeneration; preserve traversal algorithm, totals, hashes and negative-test semantics. Distinguish closure classes from gate categories. |\n| R7-5 | Low | H:13,161,497 | Header still says **r5**; profile reference points to oracle §4.3 rather than §4.2; `flush` is incorrectly included among the 13 counted prints. | Update revision/review chronology, reference §4.2, and identify `flush` as outside the gate’s print count. |\n\nConfirmed: JVM `bytes->str` uses UTF-8; JS/Dart construct characters from byte codes. PHP uses JS `parseInt` versus JVM `Long/parseLong`, establishing precision divergence within JVM long range.\n\nThe four dropped files are absent from all closure lists; the other two contain only exempt catch arms. Stored totals reproduce **142/140/141**, **133 shared**, **155 union**. UUID scopes are **27/26/21**. Waitset classification, withdrawn quoted `println`, **13** counted prints and **33** B-tree M calls match source. §2.4 correctly distinguishes direct mutations from gate-visible calls and assigns H2’s audit.\n\nThe source-cost list supports **24 edited + 3 deleted**; index is no longer “untouched.” Table A sums to **76**, with seven new profile files and at least five test namespaces. No duplicate headings, broken border sequences, em-dashes or lines exceeding 170 columns were found.\n\n**Could not verify:** runtime lane results, independent traversal execution, exhaustive ownership compliance or future migration tests/performance. No files changed.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 20:05:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 03:05:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-fable-5-1\nSession-ID: 36134bec-9cbd-4852-b78c-4901af66fd02\nTask: host-boundary design r8, astra's review of r7 (R7-1 to R7-5)\nRole: Architect\nDeliverable: docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md edited in place (r8); no src edits, nothing staged.\nLabels: V verified by reading the cited lines. No lane run. A non-Claude reviewer must confirm r8 before H1 is dispatched.\n\n# Rationale\n\n**R7-1.** No new arm is needed for PHP floats: `yin.vm.integer/decimal->float`\nexists (V `integer.cljc:769-776`, exported at `:855`, CPython `float(str)`,\nnearest-even) and `yang` already requires `yin.vm.integer`. Table B gains\n`dao.host.float/decimal-text->float` (text to digits plus base-10 exponent,\nthen that algorithm; law, overflow and refusal stated; oracle: the existing\nfloat-text cases plus five named texts) and `dao.host.text/code-points`\n(the private `data.cljc:143-159` function made public; lone surrogates are\ntheir own code point, which matches the three host calls the packet uses).\nBoth are built in H1 and are stated as not counted in the 76, so Table A,\n§4.3, §10 and HD2 keep 76 unchanged. §14 says precisely what H1 must build\nbeyond r7.\n\n**R7-2.** Four behaviour changes are now named in §10 with table D tests:\n`acopy` prevalidation; strict `utf8-encode`; `bytes->str` strict UTF-8 on\nevery host (the JVM today substitutes U+FFFD for malformed input, V, so\nmalformed bytes change on all three and non-ASCII bytes on JS and Dart);\nPHP integers above 2^53 exact on JS. H2's cell lists the consumer tests\n(non-ASCII and malformed bytes, `9007199254740993`, 400-digit texts,\ncarrier law). The public encoding arity is preserved pending **OQ11**,\nrecorded as open with the evidence (no repository caller, macro prelude\nexcludes it, JVM-only arity) and the recommendation to make it portable\nwith UTF-8 the only accepted name.\n\n**R7-3.** The index comparator keeps Dart's exclusion by construction: the\nnative path is taken for exact integers only, every float goes through\n`cbor/num-compare` on every host; signed-zero, NaN, infinity and `1`\nversus `1.0` tests are required in H2.\n\n**R7-4.** \"No tool change\" is replaced: closure classes (the `class-rows`\ndata in `closure.clj:112`) and gate categories (`gate_test.clj:444`) are\ndistinguished, classification-data edits and regeneration are permitted,\nand the traversal, totals, hashes and negative-test semantics are fixed.\n\n**R7-5.** Header is r8 with the chronology; §2.2 points to §4.2; the 13\nprints are 8 `println`, 1 `print`, 4 `js/console.log`, `flush` excluded;\nthe ring buffer's `:168` arm has one name, implementation-private under the\nseams row and the `:external-stream-seam` class.\n\n# Disposition\n\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Id    | Result    | Where in r8                                                                                    |\n+=======+===========+================================================================================================+\n| R7-1  | accepted  | Table B (two functions, laws, oracles, H1); Table A float/text unchanged at 76; §3.1 row; §14. |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-2  | accepted  | §10 four changes; table D H2 consumer-test row; H2 cell; §6 and §14 \"four\"; OQ11 in §13.      |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-3  | accepted  | §3.1 row (canonical comparison, exclusion preserved); table D signed-zero tests; §14 check.    |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-4  | accepted  | §8.1 reconciliation paragraph: classes versus categories; what H0 must and must not change.   |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| R7-5  | accepted  | Header; §2.2 reference; §3.2 print breakdown; §8.1 seams row (ring buffer), dialect row.       |\n+-------+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n**OQ11 framing.** Question: remove, keep JVM-only, or make portable the\npublic two-arity `bytes->str`. Not decided; H2 preserves it behind a\nretained arm. Recommendation: portable, UTF-8 only, qualified refusal\notherwise.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 19:06:58 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-21 02:06:58 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nModel: claude-opus-5-5\nRole: QA & Verification engineer\nPrompt: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792523065000-engineer-host-boundary-h0-retag.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\nDesign: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md (r7: §8.1 reconciliation paragraph, §3.1 new rows, §3.2 three scopes)\nRationale read: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792522578000-architect-host-boundary-r7.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\nWorktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/host-h0 (branch host-h0, on top of 9f6fc27d)\nStatus: DONE. Uncommitted edits to 5 tracked files. Nothing staged, committed or pushed.\n\n# H0 retag: the 16 `:h0-unlisted` files, reconciled with r7\n\n## Summary\n\n- No list-1 entry is `:h0-unlisted` any more. Each of the 76 entries now\n  carries `{:category :clears :reason?}` as data:\n  - 9 entries were retagged to `:migration-source`, cleared by H2. Two of\n    these name their behaviour change.\n  - 6 entries were retagged to `:dialect-arm` (`:stays`).\n  - 1 entry (`rpc.cljc`) was retagged to `:deferred-scope` (`:stays`,\n    deferred entropy).\n- The gate now **fails if a list-1 file has an unexplained category**, a\n  clearing slice its row does not allow, or a closure class of `:none`.\n  A probe confirms the failure (below).\n- The six names that could not be found are **not** on list 1. They never\n  were: the generated list is the observed set, and the test asserts that\n  equality.\n- Unchanged:\n  - totals 142/140/141 with 133 shared\n  - the traversal and its independent agreement\n  - both negative tests and the scan-failure test\n  - the corpus hash set (`--check` exits 0)\n  - the 21-site list 2\n- `dao.host.gate-test` passes: **10 tests, 293 assertions** (was 10 and 65).\n  The +228 are the new per-file checks (76 files × 3). No deftest was\n  added.\n\n## What changed, per file (paths relative to the worktree)\n\n- **`test/dao/host/gate_test.clj`**\n  - **`list-1-categories` (new data):** the seven §8.1 rows of r7\n    (`:migration-source`, `:profile`, `:runtime-prereq`, `:codec-track`,\n    `:dialect-arm`, `:seam-driver`, `:deferred-scope`). For each row it\n    records the row text and the slices allowed to clear it (`\"H2\"`,\n    `\"H3\"`, `\"H4\"` or `:stays`).\n  - **`list-1`:** each value is now `{:category :clears :reason}` instead\n    of a bare keyword, built with a small `entry` helper.\n    - The 16 former `:h0-unlisted` entries carry their site and reason.\n    - The original migration sources now record the slice that clears\n      them:\n\n      | File | Clears | Reason |\n      |---|---|---|\n      | `integer/host` | H2 | deleted |\n      | `jing/cbor` | H2 | writer H4 |\n      | `jing/cbor/boring` | H4 | |\n      | `cbor/cljd.cljd` | H4 | |\n      | `jing.cljc` | H3 | |\n      | `sign` | `:stays` | `generate` |\n      | `base64` | H3 | |\n      | `debruijn` | H2 | |\n      | `debruijn_code` | H2 | |\n      | `data` | H2 | |\n      | `values` | H2 | |\n      | `chunks` | H2 | |\n      | `jing/dht` | H2 | |\n      | `linker` | H2 | |\n      | `datagram`