{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '637,660p;785,820p;880,983p;1120,1165p;1260,1300p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; sed -n '460,470p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md\"","aggregated_output":"- `dao.host.float/decimal-text->float` (P): the double nearest to the\n  decimal text `digits[.digits]`, ties to even, by splitting the text into\n  its digit string and a base-10 exponent (minus the fraction length) and\n  calling `yin.vm.integer/decimal->float` (V `integer.cljc:769-776`, the\n  CPython `float(str)` algorithm, exported at `:855`; `yang` already\n  requires `yin.vm.integer`, V `yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc`). Law:\n  equals IEEE round-to-nearest-even of the exact decimal; answers `+inf`\n  past the double range and `0.0` below it, never a refusal for a\n  well-formed text; refuses a text that is not `digits[.digits]`. Oracle:\n  `integer_test.cljc` `float-text-named-cases-test` and\n  `int_contract_test.cljc` `float-rows-recompute-on-this-host` (V, through\n  `decimal->float`) plus `dao.host.float-test` cases for `\"1.5\"`, `\"0.1\"`,\n  `\"9007199254740993.0\"` (rounds to `9007199254740992.0`), a 400-digit\n  integer text (`+inf`), and `\"1.\"`. `yang/php.cljc:95-97` switches to it\n  in H2; since `Double/parseDouble`, `js/parseFloat` and Dart\n  `double.parse` are all nearest-even on this grammar (R), the float path\n  is not a behaviour change; the integer path at `:98-100` is (§10).\n\n**Table C: runtime prerequisites (documented and verified by existing\nsuites; not profile entries; not a boundary).**\n\n+----------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+---------+\n| Prerequisite                     | Where, and the law a port must meet (V)                                                        | Slice   |\n+==================================+================================================================================================+=========+\n   into the same instance.\n2. Load the adopted closure with a reader feature only where §3.3 syntax\n   requires it; the H0 gate proves zero source edits outside `dao.host.*`\n   and the §9 deferred files.\n3. The deferred seams (§9) remain per-host work until their design lands;\n   until then a port supplies them as today's hosts do, by reader arms in\n   the deferred files, which H0's allowlist permits.\n\n## 6. Verifiability\n\nByte-level oracles exist for every hot primitive (table D). H0's gate has\ntwo lists (files permitted non-exempt arms, §8.1; resolved effect calls\npermitted) and two negative tests; it scans recursively and treats reading\nzero files as a failure distinct from zero matches, as does its library\nmode (§8.2) for the H2 and H3 host-library gates. Every H1-H4 acceptance is\nbyte-identical against a named corpus with errata preserved; the four\nbehaviour changes (§10) each have a direct test (table D).\n\n## 7. Consequence for wasm\n\nPrimitives execute in-instance. The import module `dao.stream` has exactly\nthe seven names of `dao.stream.wasm.md:38-46` (V); a primitive import is a\nboundary crossing and a violation of §2.2 whatever its purity. The gate that\nasserts a binary's import list is part of the deferred conformance suite\n(§9.4); until then the rule is stated here and checked by review.\n\n## 8. Slices H0-H4 (commissionable)\n\nExpand, migrate, contract. Each slice lands alone under the quick tier on\nthree lanes with recorded test and assertion counts; \"unchanged\" means equal\ncounts plus any added test named. The slow tier runs once after H4.\n\n+-------+--------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Slice | Phase  | Change                                                                         | Falsifiable acceptance                                            |\n+=======+========+================================================================================+===================================================================+\n| H0    | freeze | No `src/` change. (a) `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (over                | Gate passes on the current tree; the committed closure output     |\n\n### 8.1 List 1: the files permitted non-exempt host arms, baseline and final\n\nThe gate never promises \"no arms across `src/cljc`\", only \"no non-exempt\narms outside list 1\". List 1 is generated at H0 by the gate from the actual\nclosure (every closure file holding a non-exempt arm, §3.3) and must equal\nthe **H0 baseline** below, by category, with each file's reason; a\ndifference either way is an H0 finding to resolve before H1. The baseline\nis today's tree: the seven `dao.host.*` files are **planned additions**\n(five in H1, two in H3) and are not part of the H0 baseline; they are\nmarked as such in the table.\n\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Category (reason)          | Files (V, from §1 and the closure)                                                              | After H4                    |\n+============================+=================================================================================================+=============================+\n| H1-H4 migration sources    | `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`,                  | Removed from list 1 as each |\n|                            | `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd` (the Dart Jing writer; a lane-specific file counted here,    | slice lands; `sign.cljc`    |\n|                            | not under seams), `dao/jing.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/sign.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`,           | stays for `generate` only;  |\n|                            | `yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc`, `yin/vm/data.cljc`, `yin/vm/values.cljc` (table C blocks stay, as  | three of these files are    |\n|                            | exempt protocol syntax), `dao/stream/chunks.cljc`, `dao/stream/base64.cljc`,                    | deleted (§8.1 paragraph).   |\n|                            | `dao/jing/dht.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker.cljc`, `dao/stream/datagram.cljc` (bytes arms only); and,   |                             |\n|                            | from H0's finding (§3.1, profile consumers, H2): `yin/vm.cljc:404`, `dao/space/index.cljc:78`,  |                             |\n|                            | `yin/repl/query.cljc:460`, `yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc:32`, `yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:282`,         |                             |\n|                            | `yang/php.cljc:95,98`, `yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc:439`,                                |                             |\n|                            | `yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc:130`, `yang/antlr/packet.cljc:67`.                       |                             |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Profile (the arms belong   | Baseline: `dao/data/arrays.cljc`. Planned additions, not in the H0 baseline:                    | Stay; the seven additions   |\n| here by design)            | `dao/host/{integer,float,number,bytes,text,hash,sign}.cljc` (P; five join list 1 at H1, two at  | are on list 1 from the      |\n|                            | H3).                                                                                            | slice that creates them.    |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Retained runtime           | `dao/data/btree.cljc` (atomic flag `:102-119`, weak and soft references `:212-245`: substantive | Stay, with the table C      |\n| prerequisites (table C)    | arms, not protocol syntax, retained by design); `dao/data/btree/storage.cljc` (promise facade,  | reason recorded in the      |\n|                            | deferred §9.1); `dao/pretty.cljc` (`StringWriter`/pprint, display only, §3.1).                  | gate's list.                |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Codec-track files          | `dao/stream/ws.cljc`, `transit.cljc`, `cbor.cljc`, `cbor/boring.cljc`, `codec.cljc`. (H0:       | Stay; owned by that track;  |\n| (untouched by this track)  | `remote_channel.cljc` and `ws_project.cljc` carry only exempt `catch` arms and are not on the   | `codec.cljc:45` after C4.   |\n|                            | generated list; `jing/stream.cljc` is not in the closure. All three remain codec-track files    |                             |\n|                            | under §8.3; they are simply not list-1 entries.)                                                |                             |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Dialect arms (H0 finding,  | `dao/space/query.cljc` (`:808`), `yang/clojure.cljc` (`:375`), `yin/vm/engine.cljc` (`:929`): a | Stay.                       |\n| retained)                  | runtime type name in diagnostic text; `yin/repl/adapter.cljc` (`:58`), `yin/repl/dht.cljc`      |                             |\n|                            | (`:104`): the dialect's EDN reader under two namespaces. Five files.                            |                             |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Seams, stream              | `src/clj`, `src/cljs`, `src/cljd` seam and shadow files (§3.1 lane-specific list) **except**    | Stay, except the Dart Jing  |\n| implementations and driver | `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`, which is a Jing writer H4 deletes, not a seam; it is on the | writer, deleted in H4.      |\n|                            | baseline under the first row's `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc` twin. `dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc`   |                             |\n|                            | (`:168`, the in-memory stream implementation's own host atom test: an                           |                             |\n|                            | **implementation-private** arm under §4.1, its one name in this document and in the closure     |                             |\n|                            | class `:external-stream-seam`). `yin/repl/main.cljc`, `yin/repl/host.cljc`,                     |                             |\n|                            | `yin/repl/state.cljc`.                                                                          |                             |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n| Deferred-scope files (§9)  | `dao/jing/file.cljc`, `dao/space/store/fs.cljc`, `dao/space/store.cljc`,                        | Stay until the deferred     |\n|                            | `dao/stream/journal/file.cljc`, `dao/jing/coordinate.cljc`, `dao/space/dht.cljc`,               | design lands.               |\n|                            | `dao/stream/rpc.cljc` (`:114`, the `rand` arm), `yin/repl.cljc`,                                |                             |\n|                            | `yang/python/antlr/parser.cljc` and the other `yang/python/antlr/*.cljc` with arms,             |                             |\n|                            | `dao/stream/journal.cljc` (`locking`), `dao/space/transactor.cljc`, `yin/vm/ucf/authority.cljc` |                             |\n|                            | (`locking`). (H0: `dao/jing/content/ async.cljc`, `dao/stream/udp.cljc` and                     |                             |\n|                            | `dao/space/schema.cljc` are not in the closure and are dropped from list 1; the deferred design |                             |\n|                            | records them, §9.) Every remaining closure file whose arms are only `catch`/hint/protocol       |                             |\n|                            | syntax is **not** on list 1 (exempt by pattern).                                                |                             |\n+----------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----------------------------+\n\n**Baseline versus additions and deletions.** The H0 baseline is the set of\n**existing** files above (every entry except the planned additions named\nin the profile row). The planned additions are the seven `dao/host/*.cljc`\nfiles (five in H1, two in H3), which join list 1 when their slices land. The deletions are\nthree: `yin/vm/integer/host.cljc` (H2), `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc` (H4, the\nJVM/JS Boring wrapper) and `src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd` (H4, the Dart\nJing writer, a lane-specific file that the seams row excludes and the first\nrow lists, so that the lane-specific category cannot silently retain it).\nThe final list 1 after H4 is therefore: the baseline minus the first row\n(except `sign.cljc` for `generate`), both deleted Jing writers included in\nthat subtraction, plus the seven profile files. The host-library gate of\n§8.2 is consistent with it.\n\n**Reconciliation with H0's measurement (r7, V).** H0 generated list 1 as\n76 files: 60 matching the rows above after the corrections now in them,\nplus 16 tagged `:h0-unlisted`. The 16 are now categorised: nine in the\nfirst row (profile consumers, H2), five in the \"dialect arms\" row\n(retained), one (`ringbuffer.cljc`) in the seams row, one (`rpc.cljc`) in\nthe deferred row. **Two vocabularies, kept distinct (R7-4)**: a **closure\nclass** (the `:classes` set on each file in the committed edn, generated\nfrom the `class-rows` data in `closure.clj:112`, V) answers \"what host\ncontact does this file have\" per §3.1; a **gate category** (the\n`list-1-categories` of `gate_test.clj:444`, V) answers \"why is this file\npermitted a non-exempt arm\" per §8.1. The mapping is many-to-one and the\ngate test asserts it. **What H0 must change**: edit the classification\n**data** (the `class-rows` of `closure.clj`, which the worktree already\nextends with `:profile-consumer` and `:dialect-arm`, and the gate's list 1\ncategories) so that no entry is `:h0-unlisted`, then regenerate the edn\noutputs; drop nothing (the six names H0 could not find were never on the\ngenerated list, and the rows above no longer name them). **What H0 must\nnot change**: the traversal algorithm and its resolution rule, the totals\n142/140/141 and 133 shared, the independent-traversal agreement, the two\nnegative tests' semantics, the scan-failure test and the corpus hash set;\nthe lanes need not be re-run beyond `dao.host.gate-test` and the tool's\n`--check`. **How H1 and H2 clear\nthe tag**: H1 adds no consumer, so the nine stay tagged `:migration-source`;\nH2 migrates them with the other consumers and removes them from list 1 in\nthe same commit, which the `list-1-equals-the-observed-arms` test enforces.\nThe tool lives at `src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj` (H0's justified\ndeviation: the repo keeps dev tools under `src/dev/`), and the design's\nearlier `dev/host_boundary/` path is read as that.\n\nIntent: the contract's outcome tables through a scripted seam against every\nimplementation; a gate asserting a wasm binary's import list against the\nseven names. Required input: which implementations declare which outcome\nsubset (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces).\n\n## 10. Cost and what would reverse this (H0-H4)\n\nCost (P, from §1 and §8.3, revised r7 for H0's finding): **24 existing\nfiles edited** (the 16 of r5 plus the eight profile consumers H0 found:\n`dao/space/index.cljc`, `yin/repl/query.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/closure.cljc`,\n`yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`, `yang/php.cljc`, `yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc`,\n`yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc`, `yang/antlr/packet.cljc`; each a\nrequire plus one arm, so `dao.space.index` and the UCF rows file are no\nlonger \"untouched\") and **3 deleted**\n(`yin/vm/integer/host.cljc`, `dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc`,\n`src/cljd/dao/jing/cbor/cljd.cljd`), **7** `dao.host.*` files added (five in\nH1, two in H3); **at least 5** test namespaces added (`gate-test` in H0,\n`conformance-test`, `bytes-test`, `text-test` in H1, `hash-test` in H3),\nthe 3 Python integer tests and `integer_test` re-pointed; about 10^3 tests\nrerun per lane (R: JVM quick tier about 4,000); about 10^1 rewritten.\nBase64 and the Jing writer are replacements, not code motion, and are\naccepted only by their byte oracles. No performance figure is claimed; H4\nmeasures.\n\nRisk, revised (reviews N4 and R7-2): the laws of §2.4 and table A are\n**not verbatim extraction** in **four** places, and each is a named\nbehaviour change with a direct test (table D): (1) `acopy` prevalidation on\nJS and Dart (H2); (2) strict `utf8-encode` refusal where today's helpers\nsubstitute (H1 law, H3 consumers); (3) `bytes->str` becomes strict UTF-8 on\nevery host (H2): today JS and Dart build one char per byte and the JVM\ndecodes UTF-8 **with U+FFFD substitution for malformed input** (V\n`vm.cljc:404-408`; `String.` with a charset replaces malformed sequences),\nso non-ASCII bytes change on JS and Dart and malformed bytes change on all\nthree; (4) PHP integer literals above 2^53 become exact on JS (H2). The\n`aset` return value is kept as Clojure's: unchanged, stated only so the law\ncannot be read as changing it. H1 medium: new namespaces plus two new laws,\ntwo table B functions and their tests.\nH2 medium: seventeen consumers (nine of r5 plus the eight H0 found, two of\nthem with named behaviour changes, §3.1), the Jing carrier move, the arrays\nprevalidation change and the M-caller audit in one slice; mitigated by the corpus, the\npre-deletion closure-tool gate and splitting the slice per namespace if the report\nshows drift. H3 low-to-medium: the UTF-8 behaviour change is observable only\non unpaired-surrogate strings, which the codec already refuses. H4\nlow-to-medium: the corpus decides; performance unknown until measured. The\nClojureDart traps are named in §14.\n\nToday: \"A host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary;\nimplementations do not add contracts.\"\n\nProposed: \"A host boundary that can be an IO action is the dao.stream\nabstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts. A pure, total,\nsynchronous host function is a primitive-profile entry, not a boundary\n([datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) §8).\"\n\n## 13. Open owner decisions\n\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| Id   | Question                                                                                             | Architect's recommendation (not a decision)        |\n+======+======================================================================================================+====================================================+\n| OQ7  | H4's measurement: what regression, if any, is acceptable for the portable Jing writer against Boring | Accept any regression under corpus identity; set a |\n|      | on the JVM and Node.                                                                                 | bound after H4's numbers exist, not before.        |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ8  | Approve the §12 proposal (the ABI's new §8 and the one-sentence change to `datom.world.md:70`).      | Approve after the owner revises the proposed       |\n|      |                                                                                                      | wording; §2 stands either way as this document's   |\n|      |                                                                                                      | reading.                                           |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n| OQ11 | The public encoding arity of the yin primitive `bytes->str` (`yin/vm.cljc:403-409`, V): today a      | Make it portable: accept `\"UTF-8\"` (any case)      |\n|      | JVM-only two-arity `[b enc]` that decodes with any Java charset; JS and Dart have the one-arity      | through `utf8-decode`, answer a qualified refusal  |\n|      | only, so a guest program using it runs on one host. Remove it, keep it JVM-only, or make it portable | for any other name on every host. Evidence: no     |\n|      | with UTF-8 the only accepted name? Not decided here (review R7-2: absence of repository callers does | caller in `src` or `test` (V, grep); the macro     |\n|      | not establish compatibility for a public primitive). Until decided, H2 preserves the arity as it is  | prelude excludes it (`macro.cljc:666`, V); a       |\n|      | on the JVM, behind a retained arm on list 1.                                                         | one-host primitive contradicts the universal AST.  |\n+------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------+\n\nOQ1-OQ6 are closed (§11.1). OQ9 and OQ10 belong to the deferred design\n(§9.1). OQ11 does not block H1; H2 preserves the arity until it is decided.\n\n## 14. For a successor orchestrator\n\nDecided (HD1-HD5, OQ1-OQ6, the split): the rule, the exact profile with\nownership laws, the H0-H4 sequence and gates, the deferral. Open: OQ7, OQ8,\nOQ11; none blocks H0 or H1; OQ7 is needed before H4; OQ11 before H2 may\ndrop or port the encoding arity (until then H2 preserves it). **What H1\nmust build beyond r7**: the two table B functions `dao.host.text/code-points`\nand `dao.host.float/decimal-text->float` with their tests (table D); the\nprofile count stays 76.\n\n|                                                     |                                        | becomes exact on all three. The index comparator keeps   |\n|                                                     |                                        | Dart's exclusion of doubles from native compare (signed  |\n|                                                     |                                        | zero, V `:70-85`) by taking the native path for exact    |\n|                                                     |                                        | integers only and `cbor/num-compare` for every float on  |\n|                                                     |                                        | every host (R7-3).                                       |\n+-----------------------------------------------------+----------------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------+\n| **H0 finding, retained dialect arms**: runtime type | A type name for diagnostics            | Dialect semantics (§2.5): display text, never persisted  |\n| name for error text at `dao/space/query.cljc:808`,  | (`.-runtimeType` on Dart, `type`       | or hashed; the EDN reader is the same function under two |\n| `yang/clojure.cljc:375`, `yin/vm/engine.cljc:929`;  | elsewhere); the dialect's EDN reader   | namespaces. Retained on list 1 under a \"dialect arms\"    |\n| the EDN reader alias at `yin/repl/adapter.cljc:58`  | (`clojure.edn` / `cljs.reader`)        | row (§8.1). No slice.                                    |\n| and `yin/repl/dht.cljc:104`                         |                                        |                                                          |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,65p;700,790p;840,865p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '110,175p' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc; sed -n '60,110p' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc; sed -n '75,110p' src/cljc/yang/php.cljc; sed -n '395,420p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; rg -n 'decimal->float|float-text-named|float-rows-recompute|code.point' test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,65p;700,790p;840,865p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '110,175p' src/cljc/yin/vm/data.cljc; sed -n '60,110p' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc; sed -n '75,110p' src/cljc/yang/php.cljc; sed -n '395,420p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc; rg -n 'decimal->float|float-text-named|float-rows-recompute|code.point' test/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc test/yin/vm/data_test.cljc test/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"(ns yin.vm.integer\n  \"The `integer` host module: exact-integer kernels a language prelude\n   writes its integer semantics over (C3 big-integer design, converged\n   rulings 1, 2, 5 and 11).\n\n   Every export is `:pure` with declared effects `#{}`. As with\n   `yin.vm.data`, the module is not part of `yin.vm/primitives`: a\n   composition whose runtime profile names it installs it explicitly with\n   `register-integer-module`, and Yin source reaches the exports as\n   `integer/add`, `integer/parse` and so on. Guest integers go through\n   these exports only; a VM's internal counters stay VM primitives.\n\n   Carriers. Every argument may be any exact-integer carrier the host has\n   (`yin.vm.integer.host`); every integer result is in its one canonical\n   carrier: native iff within signed 64 bits on the JVM and Dart, or\n   within +/-(2^53 - 1) on JS, else the host big integer. Each kernel\n   promotes its operands to the host big integer before it operates and\n   demotes the result after, so no result ever passes through a rounded\n   or wrapped native intermediate, and equal values are `=` on every\n   host. The kernels are written once here over the shim. Under a\n   `::max-bits` of 53 or more, `add`, `sub`, `mul` and `compare` first\n   try a native fast path on operands within +/-(2^53 - 1) whose result\n   is provably exact, and fall back to the big path otherwise.\n\n   Semantics, identical on every host:\n\n   - `add`, `sub`, `mul`, `neg` and `compare` are exact; `compare`\n     answers -1, 0 or 1.\n   - `quot-rem` answers `[q r]` truncated toward zero, `a = b*q + r`;\n     `floor-div-mod` answers Python's `[a // b, a % b]`: the quotient\n     rounds toward negative infinity and a non-zero remainder takes the\n     divisor's sign.\n   - `bit-and`, `bit-or`, `bit-xor` and `bit-not` act on infinite signed\n     two's complement, so `(bit-not a)` is `-a - 1`. `shift-left` is\n     `a * 2^k` and `shift-right` is `floor(a / 2^k)`; the count is any\n     exact integer and is never narrowed before it is checked, and a\n     right shift past the operand's length answers 0 or -1 without\n     building `2^k`.\n   - `bit-length` is the bit length of the absolute value, 0 for zero.\n   - The float exports (version 3) take and answer host binary64s: a JVM\n     `Double`, a Dart `double`, a JS `Number`. `to-float` is CPython's\n     `float(int)`, nearest with ties to even; `true-div` is CPython's\n     `int / int`, the exact ratio correctly rounded however large both\n     operands are, subnormal results included and a zero quotient\n     signed by the operands; a result that is not finite answers\n     `::float-overflow`. `compare-float` compares an integer with a\n     float exactly, -1, 0 or 1, never rounding the integer: the caller\n     excludes NaN, and an infinity compares by its sign. `from-float`\n     truncates a finite float toward zero, exactly. Each is one\n     algorithm over the shim, never a host conversion of a big integer.\n   - The float text exports (version 4) use no host float parser or\n     printer. `float-digits` answers `[digits exp10]` for a finite\n     nonzero float `v`: `digits` is the shortest decimal string, no\n     leading or trailing zero, that reads back to `v`, the closest to\n     `v` among the shortest (CPython's `repr` digits), and |v| is\n     d1.d2...dn x 10^exp10; zero, NaN and infinity are the caller's to\n     exclude. `decimal->float` is CPython's `float(str)` after syntax:\n     `digits x 10^exp10` for one or more ASCII digits and any exact\n     integer exponent, rounded once to nearest with ties to even and\n     non-negative; it answers `+inf` past the range and `0.0` below it,\n     never a limit reason, and `::max-digits` never applies to its text.\n     `max-digits` answers the composition's `::max-digits`.\n   - `pow` raises to a non-negative exponent exactly, by squaring.\n   - `parse` reads an optional `-` and one or more digits of `radix` (2\n     to 36, default 10, either letter case) exactly, never through a\n(defn- decimal-exponent\n  \"The `e` with 10^e <= x / y < 10^(e + 1), for big `x, y > 0`: an\n   estimate from the bit lengths, corrected exactly.\"\n  [x y]\n  (let [below? (fn [e]\n                 (let [[a b] (scaled x y (- e))]\n                   (neg? (host/compare-big a b))))]\n    (loop [e (quot (* (- (bits x) (bits y)) 30103) 100000)]\n      (cond (below? e) (recur (dec e))\n            (not (below? (inc e))) (recur (inc e))\n            :else e))))\n\n\n(defn- strip-trailing-zeros\n  [s]\n  (loop [end (count s)]\n    (if (and (> end 1) (= \"0\" (subs s (dec end) end)))\n      (recur (dec end))\n      (subs s 0 end))))\n\n\n(defn- int-float-digits\n  [_limits f]\n  (let [v (float! 'float-digits 0 f)]\n    (when (or (host/nan-float? v) (host/infinite-float? v) (zero? v))\n      (wrong-type! 'float-digits 0 :finite-nonzero-float))\n    (let [av (signed (neg? v) v)\n          [x y] (float-ratio av)\n          e (decimal-exponent x y)\n          reads-back? (fn [d k]\n                        ;; d * 10^-k is av\n                        (let [[a b] (scaled d one (- k))]\n                          (= av (ratio->float a b false))))]\n      ;; the n-digit decimals around av are floor and ceiling of\n      ;; av * 10^(n - 1 - e); any n-digit decimal that reads back lies\n      ;; between av and one of them, so they are the only candidates.\n      ;; Nearest alone is not enough: below a power of two the rounding\n      ;; interval is half the one above.\n      (loop [n 1]\n        (let [k (- n 1 e)\n              [num den] (scaled x y k)\n              lo (host/quot-trunc num den)\n              r (host/rem-trunc num den)\n              hi (host/add lo one)\n              lo? (reads-back? lo k)\n              hi? (and (not (zero? (sign r))) (reads-back? hi k))\n              c (host/compare-big (host/shift-left-big r 1) den)\n              d (cond (and lo? hi?)\n                      (cond (neg? c) lo\n                            (pos? c) hi\n                            (zero? (sign (host/bit-and-big lo one))) lo\n                            :else hi)\n                      lo? lo\n                      hi? hi\n                      :else nil)]\n          (if d\n            (let [text (host/to-radix-string d 10)]\n              ;; a ceiling of 10^n has one digit more\n              [(strip-trailing-zeros text) (+ e (- (count text) n))])\n            (recur (inc n))))))))\n\n\n(def ^:private max-significant-digits\n  \"Digits `decimal->float` keeps before a sticky digit: a double's\n   midpoint has at most 768 significant digits (the design's 767 was off\n   by one; 800 plus the sticky digit leaves the margin).\"\n  800)\n\n\n(defn- int-decimal->float\n  [_limits digits exp10]\n  (when-not (string? digits)\n    (wrong-type! 'decimal->float 0 :string))\n  (let [e (big! 'decimal->float 1 exp10)]\n    (when (zero? (count digits))\n      (refuse! 'decimal->float :syntax {}))\n    (let [values (digit-values! 'decimal->float digits 10)\n          first-nonzero (first (keep-indexed (fn [i d] (when (pos? d) i))\n                                             values))]\n      (if (nil? first-nonzero)\n        0.0\n        (let [last-nonzero (last (keep-indexed (fn [i d] (when (pos? d) i))\n                                               values))\n              sig (subvec values first-nonzero (inc last-nonzero))\n              e (host/add e (host/from-native (- (count values)\n                                                 (inc last-nonzero))))\n              ;; keep 800 digits and one sticky digit for the rest,\n              ;; which is never all zeros once trailing zeros are gone\n              [sig e] (if (> (count sig) max-significant-digits)\n                        [(conj (subvec sig 0 max-significant-digits) 1)\n                         (host/add e (host/from-native\n   ['pow [2] int-pow]\n   ['bit-and [2] int-bit-and]\n   ['bit-or [2] int-bit-or]\n   ['bit-xor [2] int-bit-xor]\n   ['bit-not [1] int-bit-not]\n   ['shift-left [2] int-shift-left]\n   ['shift-right [2] int-shift-right]\n   ['bit-length [1] int-bit-length]\n   ['parse [1 2] int-parse]\n   ['format [1 2] int-format]\n   ['to-float [1] int-to-float]\n   ['compare-float [2] int-compare-float]\n   ['true-div [2] int-true-div]\n   ['from-float [1] int-from-float]\n   ['float-digits [1] int-float-digits]\n   ['decimal->float [2] int-decimal->float]\n   ['max-digits [0] int-max-digits]])\n\n\n(defn- arity-checked\n  \"`f` refusing, as data, a call whose argument count `arities` does not\n   admit, so a wrong count never reaches a host arity exception.\"\n  [op arities f]\n  (let [fixed (set arities)]\n    (fn [& args]\n      (let [n (count args)]\n  (when-not (<= lo i hi)\n    (refuse! op :out-of-range {::index i, ::lo lo, ::hi hi}))\n  i)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Code points\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- unit-count\n  [s]\n  #?(:cljd (.-length ^String s)\n     :clj (.length ^String s)\n     :cljs (.-length s)))\n\n\n(defn- code-unit\n  [s i]\n  #?(:cljd (.codeUnitAt ^String s i)\n     :clj (int (.charAt ^String s i))\n     :cljs (.charCodeAt s i)))\n\n\n(defn- high-surrogate?\n  [u]\n  (<= 0xD800 u 0xDBFF))\n\n\n(defn- low-surrogate?\n  [u]\n  (<= 0xDC00 u 0xDFFF))\n\n\n(defn- code-points\n  \"The code points of string `s`: a surrogate pair decodes to one code\n   point, an unpaired surrogate is its own.\"\n  [s]\n  (let [n (unit-count s)]\n    (loop [i 0\n           out (transient [])]\n      (if (< i n)\n        (let [u (code-unit s i)\n              v (when (< (inc i) n) (code-unit s (inc i)))]\n          (if (and (high-surrogate? u) v (low-surrogate? v))\n            (recur (+ i 2)\n                   (conj! out (+ 0x10000\n                                 (* (- u 0xD800) 0x400)\n                                 (- v 0xDC00))))\n            (recur (inc i) (conj! out u))))\n        (persistent! out)))))\n\n\n(defn- units->string\n  \"The host string of UTF-16 code units `units`.\"\n  [units]\n  #?(:cljd (let [sb (StringBuffer)]\n             (doseq [u units] (.writeCharCode sb u))\n             (.toString sb))\n     :clj (let [sb (StringBuilder.)]\n            (doseq [u units] (.append sb (char u)))\n            (.toString sb))\n     :cljs (let [a (array)]\n             (doseq [u units] (.push a (.fromCharCode js/String u)))\n             (.join a \"\"))))\n\n\n  [x]\n  (cond (nil? x) 0\n        (boolean? x) 1\n        (cbor/numeric? x) 2\n        (string? x) 3\n        (keyword? x) 4\n        (symbol? x) 5\n        :else 6))\n\n\n(defn- compare-numbers\n  \"Portable numeric order (dao.jing.cbor/num-compare: exact, never rounded\n   through double; -infinity < finite < +infinity < NaN), with host compare\n   kept only where it answers identically: two host integers, and on the\n   JVM and ClojureScript two non-NaN doubles (both compare exactly, with\n   0.0 and -0.0 equal). Dart's double compareTo orders -0.0 below 0.0, so\n   Dart takes the fast path for ints only.\"\n  [a b]\n  (if #?(:cljd (and (dart/is? a int) (dart/is? b int))\n         :clj (or (and (instance? Long a) (instance? Long b))\n                  (and (instance? Double a) (instance? Double b)\n                       (not (Double/isNaN ^Double a)) (not (Double/isNaN ^Double b))))\n         :cljs (and (number? a) (number? b)\n                    (not (js/isNaN a)) (not (js/isNaN b))))\n    (compare a b)\n    (cbor/num-compare a b)))\n\n\n(defn compare-vals\n  \"Compare two datom values across heterogeneous types using type-rank.\n   Numbers compare by exact value across every kind and representation,\n   with no string fallback (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, Numeric\n   identity); ties across kinds stay ties, so (compare-vals 1 1.0) is 0.\"\n  [a b]\n  (let [ra (type-rank a)\n        rb (type-rank b)]\n    (cond\n      (not= ra rb) (compare ra rb)\n      (= 2 ra) (compare-numbers a b)\n      :else\n      (try (compare a b)\n           ;; :cljd FIRST in every reader-conditional: the cljd host-eval\n           ;; pass also matches :clj, so a :clj branch appearing earlier\n           ;; in the clause list wins on cljd too — here that would mean\n           ;; referencing the JVM-only ClassCastException, which doesn't\n           ;; exist in Dart.\n           (catch #?(:cljd Object\n                     :clj ClassCastException\n                     :cljs js/Error)\n                  _\n             (compare (str a) (str b)))))))\n;; ============================================================================\n\n(defn- match?\n  [token type & [val]]\n  (and token (= (first token) type) (or (nil? val) (= (second token) val))))\n\n\n(defn- expect\n  [tokens type & [val]]\n  (if (match? (first tokens) type val)\n    (rest tokens)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"Expected \" type \" \" (or val \"\"))\n                    {:token (first tokens), :all (take 5 tokens)}))))\n\n\n(defn parse-php-literal\n  [[type val]]\n  (case type\n    :number {:php-type :literal,\n             :value (if (str/includes? val \".\")\n                      #?(:cljd (double/parse val)\n                         :clj (Double/parseDouble val)\n                         :cljs (js/parseFloat val))\n                      #?(:cljd (int/parse val)\n                         :clj (Long/parseLong val)\n                         :cljs (js/parseInt val)))}\n    :string {:php-type :literal, :value val}\n    :keyword (case val\n               \"true\" {:php-type :literal, :value true}\n               \"false\" {:php-type :literal, :value false}\n               \"null\" {:php-type :literal, :value nil}\n               nil)\n    nil))\n\n\n(defn- skip-type-hint\n           (pure 'nil? nil? [1])\n           (pure 'empty? empty? [1])\n           (pure 'first first [1])\n           (pure 'rest (fn [a] (vec (rest a))) [1])\n           (pure 'conj conj [1 2 :variadic])\n           (pure 'assoc assoc [3 :variadic])\n           (pure 'get get [2 3])\n           (pure 'vec vec [1])\n           (pure 'bytes->str\n                 #?(:clj (fn\n                           ([b] (String. ^bytes b \"UTF-8\"))\n                           ([b enc] (String. ^bytes b ^String enc)))\n                    :cljs (fn [b] (.apply js/String.fromCharCode nil b))\n                    :cljd (fn [b] (dart:core/String.fromCharCodes b)))\n                 [1 2])\n           ['require (primitive-entry\n                       'require\n                       (fn [spec]\n                         (let [ns-sym (if (vector? spec) (first spec) spec)]\n                           (effect/make :module/require {:module ns-sym})))\n                       :effectful [1] #{:module/require})]\n           ['try-require (primitive-entry\n                           'try-require\n                           (fn [spec]\n                             (let [ns-sym (if (vector? spec) (first spec) spec)]\n                               (effect/make :module/try-require\ntest/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc:569:(deftest float-text-named-cases-test\ntest/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc:578:           (call 'decimal->float\ntest/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc:625:           'true-div 'from-float 'float-digits 'decimal->float 'max-digits}\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:80:  \"U+1F600, outside the BMP: two UTF-16 code units, one code point.\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:94:  (testing \"a string is not a collection: str-length counts code points\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:186:;; Strings, code point indexed\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:189:(deftest non-bmp-code-points-test\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:190:  (testing \"one code point per supplementary character on every host\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:192:    (is (= [97 0x1F600] (call 'str->code-points (str \"a\" grin))))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:193:    (is (= (str \"a\" grin) (call 'code-points->str [97 0x1F600])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:200:  (testing \"code point order, not UTF-16 unit order\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:203:  (testing \"an unpaired surrogate is its own code point\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:207:      (is (= [0xD800 97] (call 'str->code-points lone-high)))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:209:      (is (= [0xDE00] (call 'str->code-points lone-low)))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:210:      (is (= [0xD800] (call 'str->code-points (call 'char-at lone-high 0))))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:212:             (call 'str->code-points\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:213:                   (call 'code-points->str [0xDE00 0xD800])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:214:          \"reversed lone surrogates stay two code points\")))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:215:  (testing \"code points round-trip\"\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:217:      (is (= cps (call 'str->code-points (call 'code-points->str cps)))))))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:270:(deftest code-points-str-compare-test\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:271:  (is (= [] (call 'str->code-points \"\")))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:272:  (is (= \"\" (call 'code-points->str [])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:273:  (is (= \"A\" (call 'code-points->str [65.0])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:274:  (is (= (wrong-type 'code-points->str 0 :code-points)\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:275:         (refusal 'code-points->str [0x110000])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:276:  (is (= (wrong-type 'code-points->str 0 :code-points)\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:277:         (refusal 'code-points->str [-1])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:278:  (is (= (wrong-type 'code-points->str 0 :code-points)\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:279:         (refusal 'code-points->str [\"a\"])))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:280:  (is (= (wrong-type 'code-points->str 0 :sequential)\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:281:         (refusal 'code-points->str \"a\")))\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:358:           'str-join 'char-at 'str->code-points 'code-points->str\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:511:             [(d 'str->code-points (lit grin)) [0x1F600]]\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc:512:             [(d 'code-points->str (lit [0x1F600])) grin]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:85:(deftest float-rows-recompute-on-this-host\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:418:   `decimal->float` or `max-digits`.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:423:            'float-digits 'decimal->float 'max-digits)\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc:439:            :yang.python.antlr/missing '[integer/decimal->float\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '70,105p;790,820p;865,920p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '1,60p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc; sed -n '710,777p;823,854p;1280,1299p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; git diff -- src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '70,105p;790,820p;865,920p' src/cljc/yin/vm/integer.cljc; sed -n '1,60p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc; sed -n '710,777p;823,854p;1280,1299p' docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md; git diff -- src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj\"","aggregated_output":"\n   Limits (ruling 11) are explicit composition data with no default, in\n   bits and digits: `::max-bits` bounds the bit length of every integer\n   result, and `::max-digits` bounds the digit count `parse` reads and\n   `format` writes in a radix that is not a power of two. A result that\n   would breach `::max-bits` is refused before it is built wherever its\n   size is known in advance (`mul`, `shift-left`, `pow`).\n\n   A limit breach is returned, not thrown (version 2): the call answers\n   the keyword `::bit-limit` or `::digit-limit` in place of its result,\n   and a float result that is not finite answers `::float-overflow`\n   (version 3). Every other result is an integer, a float, a boolean, a\n   string or a pair, so the sum needs no tag, and a pair is never\n   answered with one half refused. A guest prelude translates the three\n   reasons into catchable guest exceptions; the Python profile maps\n   `::bit-limit` to `MemoryError` and `::digit-limit` to `ValueError`,\n   never `OverflowError` (`py/int-result`), and its translator must\n   learn `::float-overflow`, which is CPython's `OverflowError`, \\\"int too\n   large to convert to float\\\".\n\n   Every other refusal throws `ex-info` with the message\n   `refusal-message` and ex-data built here, never host exception text,\n   never an operand: `::op`, `::reason` and the reason's own keys. These\n   reasons are `:arity`, `:wrong-type`, `:out-of-range`, `:syntax`,\n   `:zero-division`, `:negative-count` and `:negative-exponent`: a\n   caller checks for them before the call, so reaching one is a defect\n   of the caller, and it stays a host failure, never a guest\n   exception.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [yin.vm :as vm]\n    [yin.vm.integer.host :as host]\n    [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n\n\n(def module-name\n                         (host/add e (host/from-native\n                                       (- (count sig)\n                                          max-significant-digits 1)))]\n                        [sig e])\n              ;; 10^(s - 1) <= value < 10^s\n              s (host/add e (host/from-native (count sig)))]\n          (cond\n            (pos? (host/compare-big s (host/from-native 310))) ##Inf\n            (neg? (host/compare-big s (host/from-native -326))) 0.0\n            :else\n            (let [[x y] (scaled (digits->big sig 10) one (host/to-native e))\n                  f (ratio->float x y false)]\n              (if (keyword? f) ##Inf f))))))))\n\n\n(defn- int-max-digits\n  [limits]\n  (::max-digits limits))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Recognition\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- int-integer?\n  [_limits x]\n  (host/exact-integer? x))\n\n\n(defn- int-normalize\n  [limits a]\n      (let [n (count args)]\n        (when-not (contains? fixed n)\n          (refuse! op :arity {::argc n}))\n        (apply f args)))))\n\n\n(defn- check-limits!\n  \"Refuse `limits` unless both limits are given as positive native\n   integers: the module has no default.\"\n  [limits]\n  (doseq [k [::max-bits ::max-digits]]\n    (let [v (get limits k)]\n      (when-not (and (host/exact-integer? v)\n                     (not (host/big-carrier? v))\n                     (pos? v))\n        (throw (ex-info \"integer module needs explicit limits\"\n                        {::reason :limits, ::limit k}))))))\n\n\n(defn integer-module\n  \"The `integer` module's exports under `limits`, export symbol to host\n   function. `limits` must carry `::max-bits` and `::max-digits`.\"\n  [limits]\n  (check-limits! limits)\n  (let [limits (select-keys limits [::max-bits ::max-digits])\n        ;; chosen once: every fast result fits in 53 bits\n        fast? (>= (::max-bits limits) 53)]\n    (into {}\n          (map (fn [[op arities f]]\n                 (let [f (if fast? (get fast-kernels op f) f)]\n                   [op (arity-checked op arities (partial f limits))])))\n          exports)))\n\n\n(def integer-profiles\n  \"UCF 7.5.2 profiles for the `integer` module: every export `:pure`,\n   declaring no effects and no host state.\"\n  (into {}\n        (map (fn [[op arities _]]\n               [op (vm/primitive-profile op :pure arities #{} :none)]))\n        exports))\n\n\n(defn register-integer-module\n  \"Return registry with the `integer` module installed under `limits`.\n   Composition-only: the module is never in `yin.vm/primitives`.\"\n  [registry limits]\n  (module/register-host-module registry module-name (integer-module limits)\n                               integer-profiles))\n(ns dao.jing.cbor\n  \"DaoJing's canonical CBOR value codec and portable numeric operations\n   (docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md, phases J1 and J2: JVM, ClojureScript\n   and Dart).\n\n   `encode` turns one supported value into its canonical payload bytes;\n   `decode` accepts exactly one canonical payload and returns its value.\n   The byte contract is the frozen corpus test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json\n   and the rulings in its README; this namespace implements it, it does not\n   define it.\n\n   Encoding normalizes a value into a wire tree (see `dao.jing.cbor.boring`)\n   and lets Boring write the bytes:\n\n   * keywords and symbols: tag 27 `dao.jing/keyword` / `dao.jing/symbol`\n     over `[namespace name]`, read from the identifier's fields;\n   * lists and finite seqs: tag 27 `dao.jing/list` over the item array;\n     vectors are plain arrays;\n   * floating point: tag 27 `dao.jing/float64` over eight big-endian bytes,\n     float32 widened exactly, every NaN made 7ff8000000000000;\n   * integers: CBOR integers, tags 2/3 beyond 64 bits;\n   * decimals: tag 4 `[exponent mantissa]`; ratios: tag 30, reduced;\n   * sets: tag 258 over elements sorted by their canonical bytes;\n   * metadata on collections and symbols: tag 27 `clojure/with-meta` over\n     `[meta value]`, outermost, after stripping unqualified :line :column\n     :end-line :end-column (validated before stripping) and omitting empty\n     metadata.\n\n   Decoding reads the bytes with Jing's own structural reader, which checks\n   every tag, frame name and payload shape before any host conversion (the\n   plan's \\\"validate before any lossy host conversion\\\"), then requires the\n   decoded value to re-encode to the identical bytes. Boring's decoder is not\n   used: its native mappings (tag-30 ratios collapsing denominator 1,\n   stringref and date tags, with-meta, a dropped trailing index frame, and\n   JavaScript numbers that cannot tell 1.0 from 1) would decide Jing bytes.\n\n   Every refusal is an ex-info whose data carries `::refusal`, one of the\n   corpus refusal classes as a keyword (`:non-canonical`,\n   `:unpaired-surrogate`, ...); `refusal` reads it.\n\n   Numbers: the JVM keeps its native exact kinds (long, BigInt, Double,\n   BigDecimal, Ratio) and adds a `Rational` carrier for denominator-1\n   ratios. ClojureScript uses safe-integer numbers and BigInt for integers,\n   and carriers for float64, decimal and rational content. Host `=` cannot\n   compare carriers with native numbers symmetrically, so the portable\n   operations are explicit functions: `num=`, `num-hash`, `num-compare`, and\n   the recursive `equiv` / `equiv-hash`.\n\n   Dart (J2): the same reader, profile and portable operations run on\n   ClojureDart. The byte writer there is the pinned `cbor` 6.5.1 package\n   behind `dao.jing.cbor.cljd`, which implements the same wire vocabulary as\n   `dao.jing.cbor.boring`. Dart integers are 64-bit ints and BigInt, floats\n   are native doubles (a distinct type from int on the Dart VM), and decimals\n   and ratios use the Decimal and Rational carriers. Every host reader\n   conditional names `:cljd` first.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [decimal? ratio?])\n  (:require\n    #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:convert\" :as convert]\n               [dao.jing.cbor.cljd :as wire]]\n        :default [[dao.jing.cbor.boring :as wire]]))\n| integral                        | `float-refusals-test`; `int_contract_test.cljc`: `float-rows-recompute-on-this-host`; `data_test.cljc`: |     |\n|                                 | `float-seam-test`; the corpus float cases (NaN `7ff8...`, `-0.0`) through the conformance test;         |     |\n|                                 | `debruijn_code_test.cljc` (26 deftests) scalar cases for the little-endian hex.                         |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| number: decimal and ratio       | The corpus decimal and ratio cases through `cbor_conformance_test.cljc`; `cbor_test.cljc` (21 deftests) | V   |\n| construction, access, carriers  | decimal window and zero-denominator refusals; `cbor_fixtures_test.cljc`: `equivalence-                  |     |\n|                                 | groups-share-bytes`, `every-required-refusal-class-occurs`.                                             |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| bytes: all ten                  | `dao.host.bytes-test`: round trip, slice freshness, `copy!` overlap (memmove) both directions, out-of-  | P   |\n|                                 | range refusal with **no partial write** (target byte-identical after a refused `set!`/`copy!`),         |     |\n|                                 | `equal?` on equal and unequal content, and the P-entry argument-immutability property (§2.4); H1.       |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| text: code units,               | `data_test.cljc`: `non-bmp-code-points-test`, `substring-char-at-test`, `code-points-str-compare-test`; | V   |\n| `units->string`                 | the corpus `unpaired-surrogate` refusals through the conformance test.                                  |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| text: `utf8-encode`,            | `dao.host.text-test`: RFC 3629 valid and invalid sequences (overlong, surrogate code points,            | P   |\n| `utf8-decode`                   | truncated), each asserted as a **direct refusal** (never a substituted character); unpaired-surrogate   |     |\n|                                 | encode refusal; the decode cases cross-checked against the corpus `invalid-utf8` class; H1. In H3,      |     |\n|                                 | direct refusal tests on `dao.jing/digest-string` and `sign`'s message assembly (the consumers).         |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| table B: `code-points`,         | `dao.host.text-test`: BMP, non-BMP pair, lone high and lone low surrogate, empty string;                | P   |\n| `decimal-text->float`           | `dao.host.float-test`: the five texts named in table B, equal on three lanes; both written in H1.       |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| H2 consumer tests for the four  | (1) `arrays_test`: `acopy`/`aset` no-partial-write (above). (2) H3: `digest-string` and `message`       | P   |\n| behaviour changes (§10)         | refuse an unpaired surrogate. (3) `yin.vm` primitive `bytes->str`, one test namespace on three lanes:   |     |\n|                                 | the UTF-8 bytes of `\"héllo\"` decode equal on every lane; the lone byte `0xE9` and the sequence `C3 28`  |     |\n|                                 | refuse on every lane (today: a char on JS and Dart, U+FFFD on the JVM); ASCII bytes unchanged; the      |     |\n|                                 | encoding arity on the JVM answers as before pending OQ11. (4) `yang.php`: `\"9007199254740993\"` parses   |     |\n|                                 | to that exact integer on every lane (a big carrier on JS), `\"12\"` to a native carrier, `\"1.5\"` and      |     |\n|                                 | `\"0.1\"` to the nearest doubles, a 400-digit integer text to an exact big, a 400-digit float text to     |     |\n|                                 | `+inf`; the result carrier on each host is the canonical one (`carrier-boundary-test` law). Also the    |     |\n|                                 | index comparator (R7-3): `(compare-vals 0.0 -0.0)` is 0, `-0.0` and `0.0` tie in sort order, NaN sorts  |     |\n|                                 | last, `-inf < finite < +inf`, `1` and `1.0` tie, on every lane, in `dao.space.index-test`.              |     |\n+---------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+-----+\n| text: `nfc`                     | `test/yin/vm/debruijn_test.cljc` (80 deftests) example cases (V, examples only); `dao.host.text-test`:  | V+P |\n|                                 | a pinned subset of Unicode 16.0 `NormalizationTest.txt` (every part-1 line plus the Hangul and          |     |\n|                                 | composition exclusion rows); written in H1.                                                             |     |\n+---------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------